rachel: Quick question — I'm about to open a Terraform PR to change the GPU node pool. Goal: mixed instance types (a10g + g5), a small capacity reservation for enterprise customers, and spot as default with on-demand fallback. Any red flags before I push?

sam: sounds reasonable. couple quick asks: how are you handling taints/tolerations for the reserved nodes? and what's the interruption strategy for spot? :thinking_face:

rachel: good call. plan is: reserved nodes will have taint `reserved=true:NoSchedule` and a nodeSelector on workloads for enterprise-tier. spot pool is default untainted. interruption: use node-termination-handler + fallback to on-demand ASG when spot falls below 20% capacity.

jonathan: can you paste the tf snippet? want to check the capacity reservation wiring and any IAM bits for KMS?

rachel: sure — rough snippet:
```hcl
resource "aws_launch_template" "gpu" {
  name_prefix = "redwood-gpu-"
  instance_type = var.instance_types[count.index]
  // user data installs nvidia drivers 
  placement { 
    tenancy = "default"
  }
}

resource "aws_autoscaling_group" "gpu_spot" {
  mixed_instances_policy {
    launch_template { launch_template_specification { id = aws_launch_template.gpu.id } }
    instances_distribution { on_demand_base_capacity = 0 on_demand_percentage_above_base_capacity = 20 }
    override = [ { instance_type = "a10g" }, { instance_type = "g5.xlarge" } ]
  }
  tag { key = "k8s.io/cluster-autoscaler/enabled" value = "true" propagate_at_launch = true }
}

resource "aws_ec2_capacity_reservation" "enterprise_gpu" {
  instance_count = 2
  instance_type = "g5.xlarge"
  availability_zone = var.azs[0]
  tags = { "purpose" = "enterprise-reserve" }
}
```

maria: only comment — capacity reservations are per-AZ; make sure your nodepool placement constraint uses the same AZs or you risk orphaned reserved capacity. Also add a data plane tag so billing/owner is obvious.

sam: +1. Also double-check KMS access: if any node startup decrypts secrets (join tokens, kube config), ensure the EC2 role has kms:Decrypt on the key alias you use. We had a boot failure last quarter due to missing kms permission.

rachel: thanks — will add `iam_instance_profile` with encrypted secret access. For secrets we're planning parameter store with KMS CMK (alias/riw-gpu).

infra-bot: CI build started for branch `infra/gpu-nodepool-reserve`. PR: https://github.com/redwood-inference/infra/pull/412 — terraform validate in progress.

jonathan: tough one: do we prefer a small reserved pool of on-demand instances (so predictable latency) or use capacity reservations + spot fallback? I lean reserved for enterprise tier since SLAs matter.

maria: reserved for enterprise + spot for batch/experiments. But set up automatic fallback routing: if reserved nodes are full, route enterprise requests to a dedicated smaller reserved in another AZ or fall back to dedicated capacity in Dedicated offering — depends on SLA decision.

sam: okay. from tf perspective: add lifecycle ignore_changes for `capacity_reservation` id if we ever rotate AZs. avoids churn. Also add a plan-only tag to avoid accidental apply on prod without approval.

rachel: noted. I'll add the IAM bits, taint + nodeSelector, AZ alignment, and lifecycle ignore. I'll also add a small e2e test job that boots a node and schedules a sample gpu pod.

devops-qa: Ping me when PR is up for review — I'll run the staging apply and smoke tests. Also create a short runbook for failover steps (node drain, move reserved).

rachel: PR will be up in ~30m. tagging @devops-qa and @sam for review. thanks all :rocket:

infra-bot: terraform validate passed. plan created: https://ci.internal.redwood/plan/8921

sam: will review the plan later today. quick note — consider setting `on_demand_percentage_above_base_capacity = 10` instead of 20 to reduce cost, but only if latency SLOs can tolerate slightly more spot.

rachel: good tradeoff — I'll add a var for that so we can tune per-env. last msg for now, will update thread after PR.
